     6	// Tells the Playwright preflight whether the server already listening on its port was started for
     7	// this suite. Identity is not enough: `/api/health` proves the port holds this application, and a
     8	// server left behind by an interrupted run passes that while serving no fixtures at all, so every
     9	// reserved `e2e-*` username reaches real GitHub and the whole suite fails for the wrong reason.
    10	// Only the server can answer this, since the preflight did not launch it and has no view of how it
    11	// was launched.
    12	//
    13	// Why a separate route rather than a field on `/api/health`:
    14	//
    15	// `/api/health` is public and uncached, and its own comment fixes its ceiling at "GITHUB_TOKEN is
    16	// set" -- an operational fact a production operator needs. A mocks flag is not that; it is test
    17	// configuration, useful to nothing that runs in production. Adding it would be harmless on its own
    18	// merits, since the flag is never set on a deployed server and the field would read `false`
    19	// forever. The cost is the precedent: "always false in production" becomes the accepted reason to
    20	// hang test and harness state off a public endpoint, and the next flag makes the same argument
    21	// while not necessarily being always-false. This route keeps that argument from ever being needed.
    22	//
    23	// It is 404 in any production build (`next build` sets NODE_ENV, so this covers `next start` and
    24	// every Vercel deployment, preview included). On a deployed server the answer is not merely
    25	// uninteresting -- the question cannot be asked. What remains reachable is a development server,
    26	// where the payload is one boolean about that developer's own shell.
