     1	// The cache answers a repeated username and the in-flight map shares one search between
     2	// concurrent readers of the same one. Neither touches a flood of *distinct* usernames: every
     3	// one of those is a genuine miss that reaches GitHub, against a search quota of 30 requests
     4	// per minute for the shared token. This bounds what one client can spend of it.
     5	//
     6	// Per client rather than globally, deliberately. A global counter would meet ordinary visitors
     7	// with the rate-limit screen exactly when the app is busiest, and its threshold could only be
     8	// guessed at without traffic data. A per-client window targets the one caller behaving unlike
     9	// a person, so the threshold can sit far above human pace and stop being a guess.
    10	//
    11	// Per process, like the cache and the in-flight map: a serverless instance limits only the
    12	// requests it serves and starts empty on every cold start, so an in-process limit of N is
    13	// really N x instances. This reduces what abuse costs; it is not a quota guarantee, and
    14	// nothing here should be read as a global ceiling. See docs/production.md.
    15	//
    16	// The limiter never learns who a client is. It is handed an opaque key -- a salted hash, from
